@@s011 Inner product
@@s012 In the slides: "Inner product, the king of the course". From it come lengths, angles, projections, the product of matrices, linear models, correlation, convolution and neurons. Here: the definition, the geometric reading, the sign, the cosine, the projection, the link with distance and with models.
@@s013 The definition: pairwise products, then sum
@@s014 The <b>inner product</b> (<i>inner product</i>, or <i>dot product</i>) takes two vectors of the <b>same dimension</b> and returns a <b>number</b>. The components in the same position are multiplied and the products are summed:
@@s015 Definition
@@s016 <span class="m">u · w = u₁w₁ + u₂w₂ + … + uₙwₙ = Σᵢ uᵢwᵢ</span>. It is also written <span class="m">uᵀw</span> (row by column) or ⟨u, w⟩.
@@s017 Symbol by symbol: the dot "·" between two vectors is the inner product; Σᵢ is the sum over all the positions i; uᵢwᵢ is the product of the components in position i. With vectors of different dimensions it is not defined: some components would be left without a partner.
@@s018 It serves for
@@s019 Formula
@@s020 In ML
@@s021 <b>length</b>
@@s022 norms, errors, distances
@@s023 <b>angle, similarity</b>
@@s024 <span class="m">cos θ = (u · w) / (‖u‖ ‖w‖)</span>
@@s025 cosine similarity
@@s026 <b>orthogonality</b>
@@s027 orthonormal bases, residuals of least squares
@@s028 <b>projection</b> of w onto u
@@s029 least squares, PCA
@@s030 <b>weighted sum</b>
@@s031 regression, the neuron of a network
@@s032 <b>hyperplane</b>
@@s033 line, plane, decision boundary (<a href="w02-iperpiani.html">hyperplanes</a>)
@@s034 [4, 2] · [1, 3]
@@s035 The geometric formula: lengths and angle
@@s036 The same number has a geometric reading:
@@s037 θ (theta) is the angle between the two arrows, and cos θ its cosine: it equals 1 when the angle is 0°, 0 at 90°, −1 at 180°. The inner product is therefore "length times length times how aligned they are".
@@s038 Turning the formula around gives the <b>angle</b> between two vectors, which in 50 dimensions could not be measured with a protractor: <span class="m">cos θ = (u · w) / (‖u‖ ‖w‖)</span>, and then θ = arccos(…) (in NumPy <code>np.arccos</code>, which returns radians; <code>np.degrees</code> converts them).
@@s039 u · w = ‖u‖ ‖w‖ cos θ: 45°
@@s040 The sign and orthogonality
@@s041 Lengths are always positive, so the <b>sign</b> of the inner product is the sign of cos θ, and it tells at once what the angle is like:
@@s042 <b>positive</b>: acute angle, the vectors point the same way. <b>Zero</b>: right angle, the vectors are <b>orthogonal</b> (perpendicular). <b>Negative</b>: obtuse angle, they point in opposite directions.
@@s043 Orthogonality is the most important: <span class="m">u · w = 0 ⇔ u ⊥ w</span> (the symbol ⊥ is read "perpendicular to"). It is used to recognise orthogonal bases (week 6), to describe the residuals of least squares (week 5) and orthogonal matrices (week 3).
@@s044 Acute, right, obtuse
@@s045 Cosine similarity
@@s046 Dividing the inner product by the two lengths leaves only the cosine: the <b>cosine similarity</b> (<i>cosine similarity</i>), a number always between −1 and 1.
@@s047 1 means the same direction (even if one arrow is much longer), 0 perpendicular directions, −1 opposite directions. It is used when the <b>size</b> of a vector does not matter but its <b>direction</b> does: two identical poses filmed at different distances, two texts with the same words in equal proportions but of different length.
@@s048 The comparison with the Euclidean distance of the previous page: the distance says "how far apart the two points are", the cosine "how much they point the same way". They are two different questions, and the choice depends on the problem (slide 42: "when are features most similar?").
@@s049 Same direction, perpendicular, opposite
@@s050 The projection of a vector onto another
@@s051 The <b>projection</b> of w onto the direction of u is the part of w that "goes along" u: its shadow on u. It is computed like this:
@@s052 The number in brackets says how many times u fits in the shadow; multiplied by u it gives a vector on the line of u. What remains, w − projection, is always <b>perpendicular</b> to u: every vector decomposes into a part along u and a part orthogonal.
@@s053 It is one of the most used ideas in the course: in least squares the best prediction is the projection of the data onto the space of possible predictions, and the error that remains is orthogonal; in PCA the data are projected onto the principal directions. It is also exercise 3 of the test (<a href="nozioni-esercizi-test.html">exercises by hand</a>).
@@s054 The shadow of w onto u
@@s055 Length and distance from the inner product
@@s056 The <b>length</b> is an inner product in disguise: v · v = v₁² + v₂² + …, so <span class="m">‖v‖ = √(v · v)</span>.
@@s057 The <b>distance</b> too: expanding (a − b) · (a − b) as one expands (a − b)² between numbers gives
@@s058 Read in this way: for equal lengths, the larger the inner product a · b (aligned vectors), the smaller the distance. It is the link between distance and inner product that week 1 asks you to be able to explain, and the reason why "near" and "similar" go together.
@@s059 Only inner products: ‖a − b‖ with a = [4, 2], b = [1, 3]
@@s060 The linear model is an inner product
@@s061 A linear model with n features computes <span class="m">y = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ</span>: each input multiplied by its weight, then the sum. It is exactly <span class="m">y = w · x</span>.
@@s062 The slides draw it as a graph (slide 16 of lecture 2, "inner product in graphical notation"): the inputs are nodes, each edge carries a weight and multiplies, the output node sums. This drawing is a <b>neuron</b> (without activation function): the networks of week 14 are many such nodes, placed in layers.
@@s063 The linear model is an inner product
@@s064 In the slides: "Node y: weighted sum. Edges: multiply input by weight".
@@s065 Each input multiplied by its weight, then the sum.
@@s066 With enough pairs (x, y) one writes a system of linear equations in w: weeks 3–5.
@@s067 Nodes, weighted edges, sum
@@s068 The polynomial as an inner product
@@s069 A polynomial too can be written as an inner product (slide 49 of lecture 1, slide 18 of lecture 2). The trick is to put in the vector of features not x, but its <b>powers</b>:
@@s070 The vector [1, x, x², x³] is often denoted φ(x) (phi of x): a function that transforms a number into a vector of features. The powers are computed from the data, so they are <b>known numbers</b>; the unknowns remain the weights, which appear only multiplied and summed. The model is <b>linear in the parameters</b>, and it is learned with the same tools as the line. It is the idea that in week 5 becomes the <i>kernel</i> (<a href="w05-kernel.html">transforming the features</a>).
@@s071 y = w · φ(x) with x = 2
@@s072 The inner product in NumPy
@@s073 Three ways to write the inner product, and a trap
@@s074 <code>np.dot</code> and the operator <code>@</code> compute the inner product: 1·2 + 2·0 + 3·1 = 5.
@@s075 Literally the definition: products component by component, then sum.
@@s076 <code>*</code> alone multiplies <b>element by element</b> and returns a vector, not a number. For matrices it is the Hadamard product, not the matrix product.
@@s077 Results checked with NumPy. For the hand-written version with <code>zip</code> (exercise 2 of week 1) see <a href="nozioni-python.html">Python for the exercises</a>.
@@s078 a * b versus a @ b
